.Dd October 2, 2026
.Dt UNSHARE 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm unshare ,
.Nm setns
.Nd move the caller into new or other namespaces
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sched.h
.Ft int
.Fn unshare "int flags"
.Ft int
.Fn setns "int fd" "int nstype"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A namespace is a view of one part of the system that the processes in it
share.
A process inherits its parent's namespaces of six kinds:
.Bl -tag -width "CLONE_NEWUSER"
.It Dv CLONE_NEWUSER
A user namespace maps user and group IDs and scopes privilege.
A process in a new one has no ID in it, and reads its IDs as 65534, until
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /uid_map
and
.Pa gid_map
map them; see
.Xr proc 5 .
The process that makes it is privileged in it and in every namespace it
owns, until an
.Xr execve 2
or a change of its effective user ID, after which it is privileged there
only as effective user ID 0 in the namespace.
A process of the parent namespace with the effective user ID of the
namespace's maker is privileged in it too, and a process privileged in the
first user namespace is privileged in every other.
.It Dv CLONE_NEWPID
A PID namespace numbers its processes from 1.
Its first process is its init: the processes of the namespace whose parent
ends are given to it, and when it ends every other process of the
namespace is sent
.Dv SIGKILL
and no process may be made in it.
A process sees the processes of its namespace and of those below it, by
their numbers there, and sees the parent of a namespace's init as 0.
.It Dv CLONE_NEWNS
A mount namespace begins with the mounts of its parent's;
.Xr mount 2
and
.Xr umount2 2
then change its own table.
.It Dv CLONE_NEWNET
A network namespace has no interface, loopback included.
Its sockets bind and listen, and a connection or a datagram fails with
.Er ENETUNREACH ;
.Pa /proc/net ,
.Pa /sys/class/net
and the routing socket show it nothing.
Unix sockets of the abstract namespace are its own.
.It Dv CLONE_NEWUTS
A UTS namespace has a copy of its parent's host and domain names; see
.Xr uname 2 .
.It Dv CLONE_NEWIPC
An IPC namespace has its own System V objects and POSIX message queues,
none at first, which end with it.
.El
.Pp
.Fn unshare
gives the caller new namespaces of the kinds in
.Fa flags .
A new user namespace is made first, and owns the others.
The caller needs no privilege for a user namespace, and for the other
kinds needs privilege in its own user namespace or a new one made by the
same call.
.Dv CLONE_NEWPID
does not move the caller: its next children are made in the new
namespace, and its first child there is that namespace's init.
.Dv CLONE_NEWNS
and
.Dv CLONE_NEWUSER
also give the caller its own root and working directories, as
.Dv CLONE_FS
does;
.Dv CLONE_FS
and
.Dv CLONE_FILES
copy the directories and the descriptor table, and
.Dv CLONE_SYSVSEM ,
.Dv CLONE_THREAD ,
.Dv CLONE_SIGHAND
and
.Dv CLONE_VM
are accepted as Linux accepts them.
The same
.Dv CLONE_NEW
flags given to
.Xr clone 2
make the child's namespaces.
.Pp
.Fn setns
moves the caller into the namespace that
.Fa fd ,
an open file of
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /ns ,
is open on.
.Fa nstype
is the kind's
.Dv CLONE_NEW
flag, or 0 for any kind.
The caller must be privileged in its own user namespace and in the one
that owns the target.
A PID namespace becomes the one the caller's next children are made in,
and must be the caller's own or below it.
A mount namespace keeps the caller's root and working directories.
.Pp
A descriptor of
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /ns
answers
.Xr ioctl 2 :
.Dv NS_GET_USERNS
gives a descriptor of the user namespace that owns the namespace, and
.Dv NS_GET_PARENT
one of the parent of a user or PID namespace, both refused with
.Er EPERM
for a namespace above the caller's;
.Dv NS_GET_NSTYPE
returns its
.Dv CLONE_NEW
flag, and
.Dv NS_GET_OWNER_UID
writes the user ID of a user namespace's maker.
.Pp
A user namespace may be nested 7 deep below the first, and so may a PID
namespace; there are at most 32 mount namespaces at once.
.Pp
The i386 system call numbers are 310 for
.Fn unshare
and 346 for
.Fn setns .
On x86-64 they are 272 and 308.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Rv -std unshare setns
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/musl/nsmusl.c
makes a namespace of each kind and checks what it changes: a host name, a
PID, a mount, a connection, a System V object; it makes a user, a PID and
a network namespace in one
.Xr clone 2
as an ordinary user, as a browser's sandbox does, and writes the maps of a
new user namespace.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EUSERS]"
.It Bq Er EBADF
.Fa fd
is not an open descriptor.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fa flags
has a flag not listed;
.Dv CLONE_NEWUSER ,
.Dv CLONE_THREAD ,
.Dv CLONE_SIGHAND
or
.Dv CLONE_VM
was given to a process of more than one thread;
.Dv CLONE_NEWPID
was given again before a child was made;
.Fa fd
is not open on a namespace, or on one of the kind
.Fa nstype ;
the target PID namespace is not the caller's or below it; or the caller
shares its directories and moves to a mount or a user namespace, or is in
the target user namespace already.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
A namespace or a copy could not be allocated.
.It Bq Er ENOSPC
32 mount namespaces exist.
.It Bq Er EPERM
The caller is not privileged where it must be.
.It Bq Er EUSERS
A user or PID namespace would be nested past the limit.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr fork 2 ,
.Xr mount 2 ,
.Xr uname 2 ,
.Xr proc 5
.Sh STANDARDS
These are Linux calls.
Linux also has cgroup and time namespaces, which this kernel has not, and
its new network namespaces have a loopback interface, where a new one here
has no interface at all.
Linux's user namespaces grant capabilities one by one; here a process has
all of them in a namespace or none.
